# Resolution

Fixed in commit `b9ba8144` (`Scan durable reclaim roots while queue is busy`).

The finding was valid: the reclaim worker could repeatedly consume
already-queued in-memory work through `try_take_reclaim_work()` and avoid the
`wait_for_reclaim_work()` path that performs durable reclaim discovery. Metrics
or work-created/work-finished backpressure would make the pressure visible, but
would not by itself guarantee that durable roots lost from the in-memory queue
are rediscovered.

The fix makes the reclaim worker run the existing rate-limited durable scan at
the start of every worker loop, before selecting queued work. The scan still
excludes locally deferred object-payload roots, preserving the cooldown/dedupe
invariant and avoiding the duplicate-growth issue fixed earlier. As a result,
busy in-memory reclaim traffic can delay durable rediscovery by at most the
configured scan interval, rather than starving it until the queue becomes empty.

Validation run for the fix:

```
cargo fmt
cargo nextest run -p server-core final_payload_lease_drop_retries_only_when_reclaim_metadata_still_exists
cargo nextest run -p storage durable_reclaim_scan_recovers_lost_local_queue_after_reopen durable_reclaim_scan_continues_after_unavailable_pg object_payload_reclaim_capacity_counts_dequeued_work_until_finished
git diff --check
```

# Summary
Introduced: the commit changed the reclaim worker from always using wait_for_reclaim_work, which performs durable scans, to preferentially using try_take_reclaim_work, which only pops the in-memory queue and does not perform durable discovery.
Before this commit, every reclaim-worker iteration called wait_for_reclaim_work, which first scanned durable metadata roots for object-payload reclaim and bucket-delete finalization before returning queued work. The new loop first drains pending_work and try_take_reclaim_work, and only falls back to wait_for_reclaim_work when the in-memory queue is empty. The periodic durable scan helper is only invoked on the deferred-work path. As a result, if an authenticated client keeps generating reclaim queue entries faster than the worker fully drains them, persisted durable reclaim roots that are not already in memory may not be rediscovered or finalized. This can delay or prevent cleanup of stale payloads and bucket deletion finalizers, leading to disk-space exhaustion or operational denial of service.

