## Report
Validated the finding as a logic/availability bug, not a crash bug. Key code evidence: in the new worker loop, crates/server-core/src/coordinator/runtime.rs:166-185 takes pending_work, then worker_node.try_take_reclaim_work(), and only calls worker_node.wait_for_reclaim_work() if the in-memory queue is empty and the deferred-object path did not produce work. The periodic durable scan helper is only called on the deferred-object path at runtime.rs:240-247. Storage confirms the split: crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:6760-6764 try_take_reclaim_work only delegates to runtime_state().try_take_reclaim_work(), while request_ops.rs:6782-6787 wait_for_reclaim_work calls enqueue_durable_reclaim_work() on each loop. The local queue implementation at crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs:976-982 only pops an in-memory queue item. The parent commit used wait_for_reclaim_work every iteration (git show HEAD^ showed runtime.rs:105: while let Some(work) = worker_node.wait_for_reclaim_work(&worker_stop)), so the current commit introduced the bypass.

Dynamic PoC: I added a targeted unit test, poc_busy_in_memory_queue_bypasses_durable_reclaim_scan_until_wait_path, in crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs. It creates a real durable object-payload reclaim root, reopens the cluster so that root is not in memory, then repeatedly enqueues unrelated in-memory reclaim work and drains it via try_take_reclaim_work. The test asserts each post-drain try_take_reclaim_work returns None while payload_reclaim_exists for the lost root remains true, demonstrating that the busy in-memory path does not discover durable roots. It then calls wait_for_reclaim_work and asserts the lost durable root is immediately returned. Command output: `cargo test -p storage poc_busy_in_memory_queue_bypasses_durable_reclaim_scan_until_wait_path --features test-hooks -- --nocapture` => `test ... ok; 1 passed`.

Debugger evidence: lldb breakpoint at request_ops.rs:6760 hit from the PoC at local.rs:34547 during the busy-queue try_take path; a separate lldb breakpoint at request_ops.rs:6782 hit from local.rs:34566 only when the PoC explicitly called wait_for_reclaim_work, after which the test passed. This traces the two distinct paths.

Crash/Valgrind/ASan attempts: no crash is expected because this is starvation/cleanup-liveness logic. A direct compiled test run completed successfully and served as the reproducer. valgrind was unavailable in the container (`valgrind-not-found`). ASan via `RUSTFLAGS='-Z sanitizer=address'` failed because the installed Rust toolchain is stable and rejects -Z sanitizer options.

